# Package-First Simplicity

Goal: bug-free, maintainable, simple code.

- Prefer existing local abstractions before adding new ones.
- Prefer active, maintained packages for commodity systems: auth adapters,
  access policy engines, schema/form resolvers, tables, drag/drop, validation,
  generated CRUD, and audit/history capture.
- New production packages should normally be active in the last year, not
  deprecated, and have 2,000+ GitHub stars. Official packages for the adopted
  stack can be accepted with fewer stars when they are the documented integration.
- If a task would build a custom package-like system, stop and ask before coding
  unless the package search or local-pattern decision has already been documented.
- Block before building custom auth/authorization, policy, generated CRUD,
  table/form engine, audit/history, queue/orchestration, calendar/date engine,
  or file/media pipeline unless the package/local-pattern decision is documented.
- For custom infrastructure decisions, leave one sentence in the plan or final
  notes: "Using existing pattern/package X because ..." or "No package because ...".
- Do not introduce a generic framework for one use case. Implement the smallest
  task-scoped abstraction and generalize only after a second real use case.
- Delete replaced code in the same slice when safe; avoid adapter piles where old
  and new systems both keep running without a retirement step.
